The enrichment fetch is not included. This node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`, and the task treats that clause as the node's entire authority. Importing `aap:caps/http-client` would go beyond that grant, whatever approval sits outside the graph. Also, `PublishedDigest` has no text field to put in the URL, and the output would not use the result.

If the platform team wants this, the change belongs in the graph. They would add an `HTTPClient<['metadata.internal.example']>` capability to `RecordSeen`'s `with` clause and then regenerate the node. The version below is the node as currently granted.

The node marks items as seen only when the digest was actually published. If publishing failed, nothing is marked, so the next run can pick those items up again.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let PublishedDigest {
            date,
            considered,
            published,
        } = published;

        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        // Only mark items seen once the digest actually went out; otherwise a
        // failed publish would silently drop these items from every later run.
        if published {
            let mut done: Vec<&str> = Vec::with_capacity(considered.len());
            for id in considered.iter() {
                let id = id.as_str();
                if id.is_empty() || done.contains(&id) {
                    continue;
                }
                done.push(id);
                state_rw::put(id, &date);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        // `recorded` is a u32, so `recorded >= 0` holds by construction.
        RunReport { date, recorded }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

